<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Yeast</journal-id><journal-title>Yeast (Chichester, England)</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0749-503X</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1097-0061</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">10900454</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2448324</article-id>        <article-id pub-id-type="pii">S1531691200000139</article-id>        <article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1002/1097-0061(20000630)17:2&#x0003c;81::AID-YEA22&#x0003e;3.0.CO;2-Z</article-id>    <article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Proteome Profiling&#x02014;Pitfalls and Progress</article-title></title-group><contrib-group>        <contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><name><surname>Haynes</surname><given-names>Paul A.</given-names></name><address><email>p.haynes@nadii.novartis.com</email></address></contrib>        <contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Yates III</surname><given-names>John R.</given-names></name></contrib>                            </contrib-group>            <aff id="aff"><label/>                <addr-line>Novartis Agricultural Discovery Institute</addr-line>                <addr-line>3115 Merryfield Row</addr-line>                <addr-line>San Diego</addr-line>                <addr-line>CA</addr-line>                <addr-line>92121</addr-line>                <country>USA</country>            </aff>        <pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>2000</year></pub-date><volume>17</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>81</fpage><lpage>87</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 2000 Hindawi Publishing Corporation.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2000</copyright-year><copyright-holder>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</copyright-holder><license license-type="open-access" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/"><p>This is an open access article distributed under the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.</p></license></permissions><abstract>                                    <p>In this review we examine the current state of analytical methods in proteomics. The conventional methodology using two-dimensional electrophoresis gels and mass spectrometry is discussed, with particular reference to the advantages and shortcomings thereof. Two recently published methods which offer an alternative approach are presented and discussed, with emphasis on how they can provide information not available via two-dimensional gel electrophoresis. These two methods are the isotope-coded affinity tags approach of Gygi <italic>et al</italic>. and the two-dimensional liquid chromatography&#x02013;tandem mass spectrometry approach as presented by Link <italic>et al</italic>. We conclude that both of these new techniques represent significant advances in analytical methodology for proteome analysis. Furthermore, we believe that in the future biological research will continue to be enhanced by the continuation of such developments in proteomic analytical technology.</p>                                </abstract></article-meta></front></article>